p.src:
Restoring those indices gives the Riemann tensor in the convention of Eq. : $\boxed{
  \begin{aligned}
    R^\alpha{}_{\beta\mu\nu}
    &=
    \partial_\mu\Gamma^\alpha{}_{\nu\beta}
    -
    \partial_\nu\Gamma^\alpha{}_{\mu\beta}\\
    &\quad+
    \Gamma^\alpha{}_{\mu\lambda}
    \Gamma^\lambda{}_{\nu\beta}
    -
    \Gamma^\alpha{}_{\nu\lambda}
    \Gamma^\lambda{}_{\mu\beta}.
  \end{aligned}}$ Changing the order in the defining commutator changes the overall sign; changing the loop orientation does the same. The convention matters, but no geometric conclusion depends on selecting one convention rather than the other.

