p.src:
We now turn from the geometry of curvature to the motion of matter. The relativistic rule is simpler than a force law: a freely falling particle follows a spacetime geodesic. If $\tau$ is proper time, the tangent vector is $u^\mu=dx^\mu/d\tau$, and parallel transport of the tangent along itself gives $\boxed{
  \frac{d^2x^\mu}{d\tau^2}
  +
  \Gamma^\mu{}_{\sigma\lambda}
  \frac{dx^\sigma}{d\tau}
  \frac{dx^\lambda}{d\tau}
  =0.}$ With the $+---$ signature and $c=1$, $d\tau^2=g_{\mu\nu}dx^\mu dx^\nu$ for a timelike worldline.

