p.src:
The lecture stops before deriving the coefficients, but it reveals the destination: $G_{\mu\nu}
  =
  R_{\mu\nu}
  -
  \frac12g_{\mu\nu}R.$ Why precisely this combination is selected, how its divergence relates to local energy–momentum conservation, and how its normalization recovers Newtonian gravity are the next steps. For now the logic is already visible: $\begin{aligned}
    \text{geodesics}
    &\longrightarrow g_{00}\simeq1+2\Phi\\
    &\longrightarrow \nabla^2\Phi=4\pi G\rho\\
    &\longrightarrow G_{\mu\nu}=8\pi G T_{\mu\nu}.
  \end{aligned}$

